  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nWhile the robust mid/upper-level ridge will continue to weaken into \nSaturday, 850mb temperatures will be slower to cool, allowing for \ncontinued heat into the weekend. In fact, confidence in hot ambient \nair temperatures is actually the highest Saturday with LREF IQR \nspreads around 3-5 degrees centered on near record values for some. \nThis higher confidence forecast is likely a result of stronger low-\nlevel southwesterly flow that will result in two things. The first \nbeing stronger WAA, mixing, and downslope warming off the Ozark \nPlateau helping to boost ambient air temperatures downstream of the \nOzarks. Secondly, this wind direction should advect slightly lower \nmoisture (dewpoints in the 60s) into the region that will allow for \na quicker warmup thanks to a 'drier' heat. Therefore, confidence is \nhigh that dangerous and unseasonably hot conditions will continue \nfor most along/south of I-70 through Saturday.\n\nA much anticipated upper-level longwave trough slowly traverses the \nIntermountain West on Sunday. This feature is broad and has \ncontinued to trend slower among long-range guidance, which for us \nmeans continued heat. If slowing trends continue with each forecast, \nthere is potential that the Heat Advisory may need to be extended \nfor portions of the area on Sunday. This upper-level feature will \neventually aid in leeside cyclogenesis which will work to push a \ncold front across the area. It seems increasingly likely that \nunseasonably hot weather (highs upper 80s-mid 90s) will continue to \nenvelop at least parts of the area through Sunday, as this potential \ncold front trends slower. Guidance is in good agreement that the \nfront may finally reach the area on Monday with cooler temperatures \nto follow. It is still unclear just how cool the airmass advected in \nwill be but it appears that moisture will be a little slower to move \nout, which might make it seem warmer than it is into Tuesday. \nAnalyzing LREF high temperature statistics for next week in the post-\nfrontal airmass reveal that an IQR spread of 10 degrees and \n10th/90th values ranging 20 degrees indicates that confidence is \nlower in the source region and identity of the airmass. With LREF \ntemperature IQRs ranging from highs in the 70s to 80s, it is at \nleast a signal that cooler weather and a return to near normal \ntemperatures are on the horizon by the middle of next week.",
